// @run
// @log 5
// @log 7
// @log 9
// @log 2.5
// @log 11
// A type PARAMETER whose name is a PRIMITIVE binds NOTHING — the checker resolves the
// annotation `primTyOfName -> tpEnvTyOfName -> declaredTyOfName`, so in
// `function id<i64>(v: i64)` the `i64` IS the builtin. `type-param-name-shadows-builtin.vl`
// pins the ALIAS and array halves of that rule; this is the FuncDecl SUBSTITUTION half,
// which `emit_base.monoTyParamOf` did not have. It claimed `i64` as a type parameter,
// `monoBindFromAnn` bound it to whatever the CALL passed, and the instance's PARAMETER was
// rewritten to the argument's type while the RETURN kept the checker's `i64` —
// `(param i32) (result i64)` in the disassembly, `vl check` rc=0, invalid wasm.
//
// THE AXIS IS THE ARGUMENT'S TYPE, NOT THE REP. Swept as a 6 x 6 parameter-NAME x
// ARGUMENT-TYPE grid against a `<T>`-named control, exactly two cells moved: `<i64>` and
// `<f64>` handed an `i32`-typed argument. Every other cell is either the DIAGONAL — where
// the argument's type equals the shadowed name, so the erasure is a no-op and the cell is
// green for a reason that has nothing to do with the guard — or a checker reject. A single
// argument literal per row measures the diagonal and calls it the answer; that is how the
// earlier filing concluded "i32 / f64 / string / boolean all run" and named the inferred
// RETURN as the anchor, which is the wrong half of the signature.
//
// Both off-diagonal cells are pinned below at a WIDE rep, together with two diagonal cells
// (which must not move) and the `<T>` control the whole grid is read against.

// The two cells that were invalid wasm: a wide-scalar parameter NAME handed a narrower
// argument. `av` is annotated rather than passed as a literal — a bare `id(5)` is an i32
// literal that widens at the call and lands back on the diagonal.
function idI64<i64>(v: i64): i64 { return v }

const a: i32 = 5
print(idI64(a))

function idF64<f64>(v: f64): f64 { return v }

const b: i32 = 7
print(idF64(b))

// The DIAGONAL for the same two declarations: the argument's type IS the shadowed name, so
// nothing the guard does may change these.
const c: i64 = 9
print(idI64(c))

const d: f64 = 2.5
print(idF64(d))

// The CONTROL: the identical body under a parameter name that genuinely binds. Here the
// substitution SHOULD run, and the annotation is pinned to `i64` anyway.
function idT<T>(v: i64): i64 { return v }

const e: i32 = 11
print(idT(e))
